  "limitations": [],
  "minimum_safe_summary": "This paper, a narrative review contextualized by a university community service program (KKN) involving room renovations, explores the significant influence of architectural psychology and communication on individual well-being and performance. It synthesizes existing literature to argue that spatial layouts, including factors like color, lighting, noise, and overall design strategy, profoundly impact psychological comfort, mental health, cognitive functions such as memory and achievement, and social interaction. Key findings indicate that pleasant, well-arranged, and acoustically controlled environments can reduce stress, enhance productivity, and foster positive emotional states. The paper emphasizes that architectural design variables must be carefully considered to optimize human psychological and physical health, thereby facilitating task performance and overall well-being. No specific limitations of the review methodology or the KKN project's impact assessment are explicitly stated."
